{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791594419,"nonce":"BB3UHj1PGVg5oAIQ7aBUmof7MQA=","title":"Inline shadows in sdf_core and sdf_notch, and circle shadows, keep the unblurred-shadow faults","message":"The box-shadow fix for e4adcec covered sdf_shadow (three variants) only. Other shaders carry the same code, read from the WGSL and not rendered:\n\n- sdf_core, sdf_core_vb, sdf_core_dt and sdf_notch, sdf_notch_vb, sdf_notch_dt draw a shadow inline for a primitive that has its own shadow fields. Each still guards it with `shadow.z \u003e 0.0 || shadow.w != 0.0` (an offset-only shadow is dropped), adds the spread to every corner radius (a square box gets rounded shadow corners), and draws a hard aliased edge when blur is 0. Their quads may not grow by the spread either.\n- shadow_circle in common/sdf_common.wgsl returns a hard step when sigma is 0, with no antialiasing.\n\nThe fix in sdf_shadow is the model: shadow_spread_radius in sdf_common.wgsl is shared, and the blinc_test_suite shadow_edges scene shows the cases. sdf_notch's shadow follows the notch outline, so its spread rule needs thought before copying the box rule.\n\nWhether a primitive with an inline shadow and no blur reaches these shaders from the renderer was not checked.","files":null}]}